  abstract = {Cerebral small vessel disease (CSVD) is a major contributor to vascular cognitive impairment. As gait abnormalities emerge early in the disease course and are closely related to cognitive decline, quantitative gait assessment offers a promising approach for the early detection of CSVD-related cognitive impairment (CSVD-CI). Dual-task (DT) gait assessment increases cognitive demands during walking and may reveal cognitive-motor deficits that are less apparent under single-task (ST) conditions. However, heterogeneity in gait metrics, imaging criteria, and cognitive paradigms has resulted in inconsistent findings, thereby impeding the identification of robust gait markers for CSVD-CI. A systematic search of PubMed, Web of Science, Scopus, and EBSCO identified nine studies reporting DT gait outcomes and eight reporting ST gait outcomes in patients with CSVD-CI, with four studies reporting outcomes under both conditions. Compared with ST conditions, DT gait assessment more effectively discriminated patients with CSVD-CI from cognitively unimpaired controls. Under DT conditions, CSVD-CI patients exhibited significantly slower gait speed (p = 0.014), greater stride time variability (p = 0.027), and higher dual-task costs (p < 0.001), suggesting a slower and less stable gait pattern. Subgroup and sensitivity analyses suggested that CSVD imaging characteristics may contribute to between-study heterogeneity, although these findings remain exploratory. These findings support further inv},
}

