  abstract = {Stroke often leads to motor dysfunction, and accurate assessment is critical for rehabilitation. Electroencephalography (EEG), as a non-invasive technique, provides objective markers of cortical activity, but comprehensive analyses across multiple frequency bands and brain regions during task performance remain limited. In this study, EEG data were recorded from stroke patients and healthy controls during resting and grasping tasks. Pairwise-derived brain symmetry index (pdBSI), relative power (RP), and power ratio index (PRI) were computed across regions and frequency bands, and their correlations with clinical scales were analyzed. Results showed that stroke patients exhibited greater interhemispheric asymmetry and altered cortical activation in the lesioned lesioned hemisphere. Both RP and PRI effectively captured group differences and were significantly correlated with motor function, suggesting their potential as objective markers for evaluating post-stroke motor deficits.},
