  abstract = {Sleep staging is very important for the effective diagnosis and pre-intervention of patients with sleep disorders. The traditional sleep staging method based on manual scoring is highly subjective, time-consuming and the collection methods are complicated and cumbersome. From the perspective of optimizing the selection of EEG channels in automatic sleep staging, we collected PSG signals from 26 subjects in the sleep laboratory, extracted the frequency domain features based on power spectral density and the time-frequency features of wavelet transform of 12 channel sleep EEG signals located in the forehead-central area. The separability and validity of the extracted features were explored by the One Way Analysis of Variance (ANOVA). The light Gradient Boosting Machine (LGB) and leave-one-out cross-validation method is used to classify the four stages of sleep: wakefulness (W), light sleep (LS), deep sleep (DS), and rapid eye movement (REM). Through channel screening in different EEG reference montages, an EEG channel selection that can be applied to wearable sleep monitoring devices is proposed. Moreover, in order to verify the generalization ability of the staging model. Sleep-EDFx, a public data set provided by PhysioNet, is used to evaluate and compare the performance of the proposed model. The experimental results show that the T4-AVG channel with ear electrodes as a reference achieves the highest classification accuracy of 92.25% and 0.857 kappa coefficient, which provide},
}

@misc{ming_dong_b59e04749093,
