  abstract = {Many recent studies have confirmed that brain activity in the gamma frequency range is associated with many cognitive functions. It has been found that patients with neuropsychiatric disorders such as schizophrenia, Alzheimer's disease, and depression have varying degrees of abnormal gamma frequency oscillations. Currently, non-invasive brain stimulation techniques such as transcranial electrical stimulation (TES) and transcranial magnetic stimulation (TMS) are commonly used clinically to modulate this abnormal gamma oscillation phenomenon. However, there are some problems with such techniques, such as contradictory effects, misuse of technology ethics, and poor treatment experience for patients. Recent studies suggest that 40 Hz sound stimulation may be a new method to modulate abnormal gamma frequency oscillations. However, whether such sound stimulation can improve patients' gamma frequency oscillation power is still unknown. To explore a simple, non-invasive, and friendly method of modulating oscillatory abnormalities, 30 healthy subjects were randomly recruited and equally divided into treatment and control groups. The 15 healthy subjects in the treatment group were periodically modulated with 40Hz sound stimulation for 5 days. The control group was not modulated to verify the effectiveness of the sound stimulation modulation method. Sound stimulation is presented through a computer and head-mounted noise-canceling headphones.The results showed that after periodic 40 Hz },
}

