  annotation = {中文辅助译名：一种用户友好的相位编码范式使用 48Hz 不明显闪烁的基于 SSVEP 的 BCI},
  abstract = {High-frequency () steady-state visual evoked potential (SSVEP) based brain-computer interfaces (BCIs) are safer and more comfortable for users. However, higher stimulus frequency leads to lower SSVEP amplitude and signal-to-noise ratio (SNR). Therefore, the high-frequency SSVEP-BCIs were seldom reported, especially BCIs using stimulus frequency above 40Hz. This study proposed a novel phase code method to encode sixteen visual stimuli using inconspicuous flickering at 48Hz. Simulated annealing (SA) method was adopted to optimize the selection of phase sequences. SSVEP features were detected with an ensemble task-related component analysis (eTRCA) algorithm. Twelve subjects participated in the offline experiment. Results showed that it was feasible to elicit phase-locked SSVEPs using stimulus frequency at 48Hz. Based on the novel paradigm, this study achieved an average accuracy of 84.659.96 and an average ITR of 75.6316.37 bits/min. Especially, the highest ITR reached 100.03 bits/min in this study, which was the highest among nowadays studies with stimulus frequency over 40Hz. These results proved that the phase code was a promising paradigm to encode high-frequency SSVEPs and to extend the number of instructions in BCIs.},
}

